Z||000006||Patrick||05-18-99||11:01 PM||||<br>Well, that's not what I was suggesting. <p>If you could change gears easily when, and only when, the engine wasn't running, then I'd say that the clutch wasn't disengaging properly. However, this doesn't appear to be your problem. You seem to have a problem shifting at ANY time. You should be able to shift gears easily enough without a clutch when the engine is not running. The fact that you STILL can't shift indicates to me that the clutch is NOT your problem.<p>When you had the shift cables disconnected from the transmission, was it still difficult to move the shifter. It should have been able to move VERY easily.<p>I'm trying to determine where the resistance is.<p>  
Z||000007||anna||05-20-99||05:34 PM||||when I disconnect the shiftcabes from the Transmisson it is very easy to change gears. 
Z||000008||Patrick||05-21-99||01:19 PM||||<br>Anna, when the shift cables are disconnected from the transmission and you say "it is very easy to change gears", do you actually mean that it is <b>easy to move the shifter</b>, or do you mean that back at the transmission that you can use your hand and <b>shift the gears</b>. This is obviously an important distinction.<p>This is slow progress, but hopefully we may get somewhere.<p><p>
Z||000009||anna||05-21-99||05:19 PM||||I can easily move the shifter, but I also easily[by hand],shift the gears at the transmisson.I do greatly appreciate your help.
Z||000010||Patrick||05-21-99||07:17 PM||||<br>Let's see if we got this right...<p>When everything is connected you can't shift gears.<p>It makes no difference whether the engine is running or not.<p>When you disconnect the shifter cables from the transmission, you can move the shifter easily, and you can shift the gears at the transmission by hand.<p>This makes NO sense unless...<p>...unless you simply don't have the shifter adjusted properly.<p>What has led you to believe that there is something wrong with the clutch?<p><p>  
Z||000011||87FieroGT||05-22-99||02:57 AM||||Sounds to me that if you are able to shift gears at the transmission with the cables disconnected, but you can't when they are connected. Then there must be something wrong with the shift cables themselves or they are not adjusted right. Also, even though it may seem that the clutch is engaging enough it may not be which would cause the problem you are encountering.
Z||000012||Patrick||05-22-99||02:05 PM||||<br>GT, you meant to say, "it may seem that the clutch is <b>disengaging</b> enough", didn't you?<p>Three of us here have now all mentioned the possibility of shifter/clutch cable adjustment concerns. Anna, I think it's worth while concentrating your efforts there. <p><b>However, this doesn't necessarily mean that you may not ALSO have clutch problems.</b><p>It would seem to me to be simple enough to disconnect the shift cables, put the damn thing in first gear by hand (at the transmission), and then drive around the parking lot. If you can stop and start smoothly by engaging and disengaging the clutch while in first gear, the clutch is alright. Just make sure that the car doesn't want to "creep" forward while the clutch is depressed. If it does, you DO have some kind of a clutch problem.<p>Anna, you say your Fiero has been in the shop for two months. Are the mechanics there totally incompetent? Or are they satisfied just to charge you storage fees?<p><br>
